Content-Type: text/plain; charset="UTF-8"

To the Linux USB/xHCI maintainers,

I have a reproducible xHCI failure on an AMD 300 Series Chipset USB 3.1
xHCI controller where a full-speed USB device experiencing repeated
protocol/address failures causes the xHCI command-ring recovery path to
remain wedged for several minutes. The eventual command-ring abort fails
and Linux declares the host controller dead.

The triggering peripheral is a Jumperless V5 composite USB device, VID:PID
1d50:acab. Its mass-storage interface uses usb-storage rather than UAS.

System:
    Kernel: Linux 7.2.6-arch2-1
    Distribution: Arch Linux
    CPU: AMD Ryzen 7 5800X3D
    Motherboard: Gigabyte AB350M-D3H
    BIOS: F52g, 2022-07-27
    xHCI controller:
        AMD 300 Series Chipset USB 3.1 xHCI Controller
        PCI ID: 1022:43bb, rev 02
        PCI address: 0000:02:00.0
        Subsystem: ASMedia 1b21:1142
        Driver: xhci_hcd / xhci_pci
        MSI-X enabled
    USB device: Jumperless V5, VID:PID 1d50:acab
    Affected USB path: usb 1-3
    Mass-storage interface: 1-3:1.8, usb-storage

The failure begins when usb-storage attempts to recover/reset the device.
The kernel reports repeated protocol failures:

usb 1-3: reset full-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd
usb 1-3: device descriptor read/64, error -71
...
usb 1-3: Device not responding to setup address.
usb 1-3: device not accepting address 2, error -71
usb 1-3: WARN: invalid context state for evaluate context command.
usb 1-3: reset full-speed USB device number 2 using xhci_hcd
xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: ERROR unknown event type 2
usb 1-3: Device not responding to setup address.
xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: ERROR unknown event type 2

After this, usb-storage remains blocked in xhci_setup_device():

task:usb-storage state:D
wait_for_completion
  xhci_setup_device
    hub_port_init
      usb_reset_and_verify_device
        usb_reset_device
          usb_stor_port_reset
            usb_stor_invoke_transport
              usb_stor_control_thread

The task remained there for at least 245 seconds.
SCSI error handling then blocks behind usb-storage:

Workqueue: scsi_tmf_8 scmd_eh_abort_handler
wait_for_completion
  command_abort_matching
    scmd_eh_abort_handler

A disk-events worker also becomes blocked trying to issue TEST UNIT READY
to the corresponding removable SCSI disk.
Eventually, several minutes after the initial failure, xHCI recovery
escalates to:

xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: Abort failed to stop command ring: -110
xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: xHCI host controller not responding, assume dead
xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: HC died; cleaning up
xhci_hcd 0000:02:00.0: Timeout while waiting for setup device command

The controller is then declared dead and attached USB devices begin
disconnecting.
I captured xHCI debugfs state while the controller was still wedged.
The command-ring software pointers were:

dequeue: 00000000fff7eb30
enqueue: 00000000fff7eb50
cycle:   1

The nearby command TRBs included:

fff7eb20  Enable Slot
fff7eb30  No-Op Command
fff7eb40  Get Port Bandwidth Command
fff7eb50  Stop Ring Command

The event ring contains 68 consecutive "Command Ring Stopped" events all
referring to the same command TRB:

Command Ring Stopped -> 00000000fff7eb40

These continue to the end of one event-ring segment and then for several
entries after the event ring wraps.
The event-ring software dequeue pointer was:

00000000fff7d030

The operational-register snapshot taken while wedged showed:

USBCMD = 00000000
USBSTS = 00000011
CRCR   = 0000000000000008

At the time of the snapshot, USBSTS indicated that the host controller was
halted, while the CRR bit remained set in CRCR.

Immediately before the repeated Command Ring Stopped events, the captured
event ring contains a sequence of failed/retried commands including Reset
Device, Address Device, Disable Slot, Enable Slot and Evaluate Context.
Address Device reports USB Transaction Error, while Evaluate Context and
Reset Device report Context State Error.

I have attached:

    xhci-wedge.tar.gz
        xHCI debugfs command/event ring dumps
        operational/runtime registers
        endpoint/device debugfs state
    xhci-wedge-kernel.log
        full kernel journal including SysRq task dumps
    xhci-wedge-lspci.txt
        lspci -vvv for 0000:02:00.0

I still have the hardware and can reproduce the failure. If additional
traces would be useful, or if you would like me to test a patch, please let
me know.

Best regards,
Blair Calderara

